Hide pathhide-matched file-backed mappings from /proc/<pid>/maps, smaps,
smaps_rollup and numa_maps.

OFF BY DEFAULT, AND EXPERIMENTAL. pathhide.c ships with an empty rule set and
nothing in this patch set seeds one, so on a stock build pathhide_match_file()
short-circuits on its first line and every guard below is dead code. Read
common/_pathhide/README.md before loading a rule: hiding a mapping trades a
heuristic signal (a recognisable .so named in maps) for a categorical one (the
accounting stops balancing), and that trade is not obviously a win.

THE /proc/<pid>/fd HALF IS GONE, DELIBERATELY

Earlier revisions also filtered fd readdir, fd lookup/readlink, fdinfo and -- on
6.6 and 6.12 -- the fd count behind stat(/proc/<pid>/fd).st_size. All of it has
been removed, for two independent reasons:

  * It was a CATEGORICAL tell. Hiding an fd from /proc does not close it: the
    descriptor stays allocated, so fcntl(N, F_GETFD) succeeds on an N that
    /proc/self/fd/N reports as ENOENT. That probe needs no knowledge of what is
    being hidden, no rule list and no privilege, and it is never true on a stock
    kernel. It replaced a heuristic tell with a certain one.

  * The fd count rewrite made every device pay. proc_readfd_count() is what
    stat("/proc/<pid>/fd") answers with, and stock computes it as one
    bitmap_weight() over the open-fd bitmap. The replacement was an O(max_fds)
    loop taking task_lock per descriptor inside a single un-rescheduled RCU
    section -- on every such stat(), on every shipped device, including the
    overwhelming majority that carry no rules and get nothing back for it.

Gating that loop on ph_nrules would have answered the second point on its own.
Removing the half answers both, and leaves fs/proc/fd.c untouched by this patch
set entirely.

Generated hunk by hunk against the real tree the fleet pins for this kernel
version:

    OnePlusOSS/android_kernel_common_oneplus_sm8450 @ oneplus/sm8450_b_16.0_oneplus_10_pro

and dry-run at fuzz 0 against every distinct fs/proc variant the manifests
resolve to. A hunk that only lands "with fuzz" can silently guard the wrong
function, so treat a fuzz report from `patch` as a failure and regenerate.

Version notes:
  * show_map()/show_smap() are wrapped rather than guarded inline: on this tree
    they open with get_pad_vma()/get_data_vma(), which hand back kzalloc'd
    copies that only show_map_pad_vma() frees. An early return placed after
    those declarations would leak two struct vm_area_struct per hidden padded
    mapping, once per read of /proc/<pid>/maps.
  * smaps_rollup iterates `for (vma = priv->mm->mmap; vma;)` with the advance
    at the bottom of the body, so the guard suppresses the accumulation rather
    than using `continue`, which would spin forever.
--- a/fs/proc/task_mmu.c
+++ b/fs/proc/task_mmu.c
@@ -391,3 +391,26 @@
 
+static int ph_show_map(struct seq_file *m, void *v);
+
+/*
+ * pathhide: decided here rather than inside show_map_vma() for two reasons.
+ * show_map_pad_vma() emits a second, synthesized VMA for a 16kB-compat padded
+ * mapping and that pad is anonymous (vm_file == NULL), so a guard further in
+ * would leave an orphan "[page size compat]" line behind a hidden .so --
+ * present in maps, absent from smaps, which is the mismatch itself. Deciding
+ * before the body runs also means get_pad_vma()/get_data_vma() are never
+ * called, so on trees where they hand back heap copies (freed by
+ * show_map_pad_vma()) an early return cannot leak them.
+ */
 static int show_map(struct seq_file *m, void *v)
+{
+	extern bool pathhide_match_file(struct file *f);
+	struct vm_area_struct *vma = v;
+
+	if (vma->vm_file && pathhide_match_file(vma->vm_file))
+		return 0;
+
+	return ph_show_map(m, v);
+}
